KamLAND has been used to measure the flux of $ ar{
u}_e$'s from distant nuclear reactors. In an exposure of 162 ton$\cdot$yr (145.1 days) the ratio of the number of observed inverse $ eta$-decay events to the expected number of events without disappearance is $0.611\pm 0.085 {m (stat)} \pm 0.041 {m (syst)} $ for $ ar{
u}_e$ energies $>$ 3.4 MeV. The deficit of events is inconsistent with the expected rate for standard $ ar{
u}_e$ propagation at the 99.95% confidence level. In the context of two-flavor neutrino oscillations with CPT invariance, these results exclude all oscillation solutions but the `Large Mixing Angle' solution to the solar neutrino problem using reactor $ ar{
u}_e$ sources.
